statement:
A straight street is a number line with buildings on it. `buildings[i] = [start_i, end_i, height_i]` says that building `i` has height `height_i` over the half-closed interval `[start_i, end_i)`: it covers `start_i` but not `end_i`. Buildings may overlap.

Describe the street with the fewest possible non-overlapping half-closed segments, returned as a list `street` of triples `[left_j, right_j, average_j]`:

- every point of `[left_j, right_j)` is covered by at least one building, and at each such point the average height of the buildings covering it is `average_j`;
- the average of several heights is their sum divided by their count, rounded down (integer division);
- parts of the street that no building covers are left out, and two segments that touch (one's `right` equals the other's `left`) never share the same average, since they would then be one segment.

Return the segments in any order.

validator:
function validate([buildings]) {
  if (!Array.isArray(buildings) || buildings.length < 1 || buildings.length > 100000) return 'buildings needs 1 to 10^5 entries.';
  for (const building of buildings) {
    if (!Array.isArray(building) || building.length !== 3 || !building.every(Number.isInteger)) return 'Each building is [start, end, height] with integers.';
    const [start, end, height] = building;
    if (start < 0 || end > 100000000 || start >= end) return 'Each building needs 0 <= start < end <= 10^8.';
    if (height < 1 || height > 100000) return 'Each height is between 1 and 10^5.';
  }
  return null;
}

